-- Legal documents, version 0.1: terms, privacy, guidelines (story S1-E14-01).
-- Generated by scripts/build-legal-migration.ts from content/legal/ (English) and
-- content/legal/ro/ (Romanian). Do not edit by hand: change the drafts and make a new version.
-- Brand values are stored as {{tokens}} that the Legal centre fills in from src/config/brand.ts;
-- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} marks lines that start only after the invited March test.
-- Important change (everyone accepts again before posting): true.
-- Drafts, not checked by a lawyer yet (is_lawyer_reviewed = false).

insert into public.legal_document_versions (
  document_id, version_label, language, title, body_markdown, is_material, is_lawyer_reviewed,
  published_at
)
select
  legal_documents.id, '0.1', 'en', $legal_text$Terms of use$legal_text$,
  $legal_text$## In short

- This is the agreement between you and the company that runs {{productName}}.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} You must be 13 or over. Yoti checks your age and we only get the result. Under-16s also need a parent or guardian to say yes.
- Your writing stays yours. You give us a narrow, free permission to host and show it, which ends when you delete it.
- A person reads every report. We may hide, limit or cut an unlawful part, but we never change your words, and we always tell you why.
- Ads are labelled, never personalised and never inside a story or a class space.
- If we make an important change, you accept it before you post again.

## 1. Who can join

**In short** (as on the board)

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} You must be 13 or older.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} When you sign up, we ask for your year of birth, and the month if we need it to work out your age. Then Yoti checks your age. We only get the result, never a photo.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} If you’re under 16, a parent or guardian also has to say yes.

**Full text**

These terms are the agreement between you and [Company name] [SRL or AB] (“we”, “us”), the company that runs {{productName}}, “An app by {{companyName}}”. Our company details are on the Contact and company details page. When you create an account, you agree to these terms. The Privacy notice and the Community guidelines go with them, so please read those too.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} You must be 13 or older. If you’re younger, you can’t sign up. We don’t keep anything you typed, and you can try again once you turn 13.

Your year of birth. When you sign up, we ask for your year of birth. If the year alone can’t tell us whether you are under or over 13 or 16, we also ask for the month. We never ask for your full date of birth. Nobody else on {{productName}} can see your age, your year of birth or your age check result.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} The age check. After you sign up, Yoti checks your age. You choose how: a quick selfie that estimates your age, or an ID document. Yoti only tells us the result (for example “13 or over” or “18 or over”), how the check was done and when. We never get the photo or the document. If the check doesn’t work, you can try again, use an ID document instead or ask for a check by a person. Until a check passes, your account is read-only: you can read and report, but you can’t publish, comment, post or message. If no check passes within 14 days of sign-up and you haven’t asked for a check by a person, we delete the account.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Stories rated 18+ and adult settings need an “18 or over” result. If your year of birth says you’re 18 or over but the check only confirms “13 or over”, the protections for under-18s stay on until an “18 or over” result comes through.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} If you’re under 16. A parent or guardian also has to say yes. You give us their email address and we send them a link. Until they agree, your account stays read-only. If they say no, or don’t answer within 14 days, we delete the account and its data, and we tell you. Your parent or guardian agrees to these terms together with you. If they withdraw their consent later, your account goes read-only and we tell you both what happens next. [OPEN: what happens to the account, its stories and its posts after a parent or guardian withdraws consent; lawyer to decide (L1, L2)]

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Google, Apple or Microsoft. You can sign up and log in with these accounts. The year of birth, the age check and, for under-16s, the parent or guardian step still apply, because these services don’t tell us how old you are.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Where {{productName}} is open. {{productName}} opens one country at a time. We work out your country from your internet connection. We never ask for it. If {{productName}} isn’t open in your country yet, you can’t sign up from there. In Australia, you must be 16 or older. [List of open countries and any country rules: link to be added]

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Teachers. Adults with an “18 or over” result can ask for a Teacher badge by confirming a school email address or sending proof that they teach. Our staff check it and then delete the proof. A Teacher badge lets you create class spaces. It is not a background check, and teachers follow the same messaging rules as everyone else.

## 2. Your writing stays yours

**In short** (as on the board)

- You keep the rights to everything you write.
- To show your work to readers, you give {{productName}} permission to host and display it. [What this permission covers: to be drafted by a Romanian lawyer]
- You can unpublish or delete your work.

*Suggested new short line, now that the full text fills the placeholder:*

- To show your work to readers, you give us a narrow, free permission to host and display it. It ends when you delete the work.

**Full text**

You own what you write. Posting on {{productName}} doesn’t change that. You keep the copyright in your stories, poems, essays, comments, posts and pictures. You also keep your rights as an author, including the right to be named the way you choose (by your pen name or your username) and the right to have your work kept whole.

The permission you give us. To show your work to readers, we need your permission. This permission is called a licence. When you save or publish anything on {{productName}}, you give [Company name] a licence on these terms:

- What it lets us do: store your work; copy it only as far as running {{productName}} needs (for example in our database, in backups and in short-lived caches); and make it available to the readers your settings allow, on the {{productName}} website and web app and, once they exist, the {{productName}} apps.
- The technical versions this needs: resizing your pictures; fitting your text to different screens; showing your story’s card (cover, title, blurb and pen name) on shelves, in lists and in link previews; letting search engines see your title, blurb and writer name only; and putting your work in the export files that you, or your parent or guardian, ask for.
- What it doesn’t let us do: sell your work; publish it in print or as an ebook; translate or adapt it; use it in our advertising outside {{productName}}; or give it to anyone else to use for their own purposes. If we ever want to do any of these, we will ask you first, separately and in writing.
- Not exclusive: you can publish your work anywhere else, at the same time, with anyone.
- Worldwide: readers can see your work in every country where {{productName}} is open.
- Free of charge: we don’t pay you for the licence and you don’t pay us.
- Companies that help us: the companies that host and run {{productName}} for us (listed in the Privacy notice) may handle your work only to do that job for us, and you agree that we can let them. They can’t use it for anything else.
- How long it lasts: for each piece of work, until you delete it, or until it is deleted with your account. Then we take it off {{productName}} straight away. Copies can stay for a short time in caches (up to [number] days) and in backups (up to [number] days) before they are overwritten. We keep a copy longer only where the law requires it, or where it is needed for an open report, a legal case or an order from an authority, and only for as long as that lasts.
- AI: [OPEN: whether anyone may use your work to train AI; see “Writing with AI”]

Unpublishing and deleting. You can edit, unpublish or delete your work at any time. If you unpublish a story, readers can’t see it any more, but we keep it safe for you as a draft until you delete it. If you delete your account, your stories, chapters and comments are deleted with it.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Forum posts are different. When you delete your account, your forum posts stay so that conversations still make sense, shown under “Deleted user”. The licence for those posts lasts as long as they stay up. If you want a post gone, delete it before you delete your account. You can also ask us to remove your personal information from a post that stays.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Your copy. You can download your stories as Word files from Settings at any time.

Reading in the browser. Until the {{productName}} apps exist, signed-in readers can read full chapters in their browser. We switch off right-click and copying on chapter text. That makes copying harder, but it can’t stop it: screenshots still work. When the apps arrive, you’ll also be able to choose that a story can be read in the app only.

We never change your words. What our staff can and can’t do to a story is explained in “How moderation works”.

## 3. You’re responsible for what you post

**In short** (as on the board)

- Only post work you wrote or have the right to share.
- Rate your story All ages, 13+, 16+ or 18+, and label fan fiction, parody and AI help when you publish.
- Don’t post anything illegal or anything the Community guidelines don’t allow.

**Full text**

You’re responsible for everything you publish, post, comment, upload or send on {{productName}}.

- Only post work you wrote, or work you have the right to share. If you share someone else’s work, you need their permission, or the work must be in the public domain (and you should say so).
- Only upload pictures you made or have permission to use. When you add a picture, you confirm you have that right. Every picture needs alt text: a short description for people who can’t see it.
- Short quotes from other works are fine for review, comment, criticism or parody, as long as you name the author and the source.
- Rate every story All ages, 13+, 16+ or 18+. Pick the rating that fits the strongest part of the story. The Community guidelines explain each rating. Readers younger than a story’s rating can’t find it or open it. If we think a rating is too low, we may raise it and tell you why. We never lower a rating you chose.
- Label your story when it is fan fiction or parody, and say whether AI helped you (see “Writing with AI”).
- Don’t post anything illegal, anything that breaks someone else’s rights or anything the Community guidelines don’t allow.
- Don’t pretend to be someone else, including our staff.
- Don’t send spam, and don’t try to trick people.
- Don’t try to get around our age checks, safety features or limits, and don’t use bots or scrapers to copy what’s on {{productName}}.

If someone tells us that something you posted breaks the law or their rights, we look at it as explained in “How moderation works” and in our Notice and action policy. You can always tell us your side.

## 4. How moderation works

**In short** (as on the board)

- Anyone can report a story, comment or message. A person reads every report.
- Every picture is checked when it’s uploaded. If the check flags it, a moderator looks at it before anyone else can see it.
- We may hide or limit a story, fix its settings or remove an unlawful part, but we never change your words.
- If we remove something or limit your account, we tell you why.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} You can ask us to look at our decision again.

**Full text**

Our own team moderates {{productName}}, in Romanian and English. We don’t use outside moderation companies.

Reports. Anyone can report a story, chapter, comment, profile, space, thread, post, picture or message, with or without an account. A person on our team reads every report. We don’t scan your writing automatically and we don’t hold anything back before it is published: we act on reports. The most serious reports, such as anything that puts a child at risk, are dealt with first. How reports work, and what a good report includes, is in our Notice and action policy.

Pictures. Every picture is checked automatically when it is uploaded. Sightengine checks for nudity and gore. Arachnid Shield checks for known child sexual abuse images. If either check flags a picture, it stays hidden (readers see “Picture being checked”) until a moderator decides. The checks only hold a picture back. A person makes every decision.

What we may do. If something breaks the law, these terms or the Community guidelines, we may:

- hide a story or chapter, or limit who can see it;
- change its settings: where it can be read, whether comments are on, its rating (we may raise it, never lower it) and its other labels;
- fix its details: category, tags or language;
- remove an unlawful part, or someone’s personal information, by cutting out that exact passage and leaving a visible “[Removed by the moderation team]” marker;
- hide a cover that breaks the rules (your story then shows an automatic cover), or hide a title on public screens until you change it;
- add a note to a story we’ve confirmed was copied from a published work, or remove it;
- give a warning, suspend an account or close it.

What we never do. Our staff never edit your words, not even if you ask us to. You edit your own text. We never write a new title for you.

You always hear from us. Every change we make to your work is recorded, can be undone and comes with a message telling you what we did and why. If we remove or limit something, or limit your account, you get a statement of reasons: what we did, the facts, whether any automatic tool was involved, the rule or law we relied on and how to challenge it.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Asking us to look again. You can appeal within six months of our decision. Where possible, a different moderator looks at it, and we tell you the result. You can also go to court. See our Notice and action policy for every option.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Private messages. Messages aren’t end-to-end encrypted, so that reported messages can be checked. Our staff read a private message only when it is part of a report, or when the law requires it.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Spaces and class spaces. Space owners and the moderators they choose can look after their own space. Teachers moderate their class spaces. Our moderators can act in any space, and what they decide comes first.

Orders from authorities. If an authority sends us an order to remove terrorist content, we act on it within one hour. If we learn that someone’s life or safety is at risk, we may tell the police or other authorities.

## 5. How we rank and recommend

**In short** (as on the board)

- Popular lists are based on likes, reads and shares.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} “Popular in your area” uses the county you picked. We never track where you are.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Items from our sponsors are always labelled “Sponsored”, and they never move a story up a list.

**Full text**

Discover is the main page of {{productName}}. It mixes several shelves, for example Popular, New, Completed and New and rising, so new writers get seen as well as popular ones.

Popular. A story’s place on Popular lists comes from what readers do with it: reads, likes, comments, shares and how many readers finish its chapters. Recent activity counts more than older activity. Private downvotes are never used for ranking.

What we don’t use. No shelf uses your reading history, and nothing is chosen from a profile of you. Everyone sees the same lists for the same choices.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} What you choose. The categories and tags you pick when you set up your account, or later in Settings, decide which shelves you see first. You can change them at any time.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Popular in your area. This uses the county or region you picked, if you picked one. We never track where you are.

Your age. You only see stories rated for your age. Signed-out visitors see only stories rated All ages and 13+.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Forums. Forum categories are pinned in a fixed order. Threads are sorted by latest activity.

Changes. We may change the mix of shelves and how much each signal counts, to see what works best. The “How ranking works” page always lists the signals we use, in plain words.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Sponsors. Items from our sponsors are always labelled “Sponsored”. A sponsor can’t move a story up a list, and a sponsored category or contest doesn’t change which stories are listed or in what order.

## 6. Ads and sponsors {{afterTheInvitedTest}}

**In short** (as on the board)

- Ads from ad networks are labelled “Ad”. Items from our own sponsors are labelled “Sponsored”.
- Ads match the chapter or page on screen. They are never personalised, for anyone.
- Ads are never inside the text of a story and never fill the whole screen.
- Class spaces never show ads, sponsored items or sponsor credits.

**Full text**

Ads and sponsors help keep {{productName}} running. Here is how they work.

Two labels. Ads from ad networks are labelled “Ad” (“Reclamă” in Romanian). Items from our own sponsors, for example a bookshop or a public body, are labelled “Sponsored” (“Sponsorizat”) and name the sponsor.

Never personalised. Ads are chosen only from the page you’re on: its category, tags and language. We never use anything about you to choose an ad: not your age, your reading, your account or your device. This is the same for everyone. “Why this ad?” next to an ad explains this.

Where ads go. Ads can appear on public pages, such as Discover, Search, story pages, the end of a chapter, your Library, forums, public spaces and writer profiles. They are never inside the text of a story, never pop up and never fill the whole screen. There are no ads on sign-up and log-in, the age check, parent or guardian consent, your writing tools, messages, notifications or Settings. There are none on reporting and appeal pages or on the legal and help pages either.

Class spaces. Class spaces never show ads, sponsored items or sponsor credits.

What sponsors can’t do. Sponsors have no say over what we publish, how we rank it or what we remove. Before any sponsor item goes live, we check it is suitable for young readers: nothing about alcohol, gambling, tobacco, dating or anything similar.

Ad networks. We only work with ad networks that show non-personalised ads to everyone. Their code must not set cookies or read anything stored on your device. If a network can’t promise that, we show our own banners instead.

Your stories. Ads may appear on your story page and at the end of your chapters, but never inside your text. Writers don’t get a share of ad or sponsor income.

## 7. Writing with AI

**In short** (as on the board)

- If AI helped you write something, add the AI assistance label.
- [Rules on AI-written work and on AI training: to be decided, then drafted by a Romanian lawyer]

**Full text**

Readers want to know how a story was made, so we ask you to be honest about AI.

The label. When you publish a story, you choose one of these: “No AI”, “AI for brainstorming or outlining” or “Partly AI-written”. The label shows on the story’s card and page.

If a story isn’t labelled honestly. Readers can report a story they think was written with AI without saying so. Nothing happens because of reports alone. If our moderators confirm several reports from different people as true, the story gets an “AI-written, not disclosed” label and you get a warning, with our reasons. We never use AI-detector scores to decide this, because they often get it wrong.

[OPEN: rules on work written mostly or fully by AI. To be decided by the founder, then drafted by a Romanian lawyer.]

[OPEN: whether we or anyone else may use work on {{productName}} to train AI, and how writers can say no. To be decided by the founder, then drafted by a Romanian lawyer.]

## 8. When these terms change

**In short** (as on the board)

- If we make an important change, we tell you on {{productName}}.
- You accept the new version before you can post again.
- Older versions stay on this page.

**Full text**

We may need to change these terms, for example when the law changes or when we add a new feature.

Important changes. If we make an important change, we tell you on {{productName}}, say what changed and why, and give you [number] days’ notice before it applies [lawyer to confirm the notice period]. You can keep reading at any time, but you accept the new version before you can post again. If you don’t want to accept it, you can download your data and delete your account.

Small changes. If we only fix a typo or make the wording clearer without changing what it means, we publish the new version with its date.

Older versions. Every version stays on this page, in Romanian and English, with its date. You can also find these pages at sign-up and in Settings.

Two languages. These terms are in Romanian and English. [OPEN: which version applies if the two ever differ; lawyer to decide] If the short version in a box and the full text ever seem to disagree, the full text applies, and we’ll fix the short version. You can always ask us.

## Proposed additional sections (not on the board yet)

These sections cover points the board has no place for. Add them to the board or fold them into the sections above.

## 9. Your account and how it ends

**In short** (proposed)

- Keep your log-in details to yourself and tell us if someone else gets in.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} You can delete your account at any time. You have 14 days to change your mind.
- We may suspend or close an account that breaks the rules, and we always tell you why.

**Full text**

Keeping your account safe. Use an email address you can get into, and keep your password to yourself. If you think someone else has got into your account, change your password and tell us. In Settings you can see where you’re signed in and sign out everywhere.

Your username and pen name. You choose a username, and you can add a pen name. Both must follow the Community guidelines. You can change your pen name once every 30 days.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Leaving. You can delete your account at any time from Settings, on any device or on the website. We switch it off straight away and delete it for good after 14 days, unless you change your mind before then. Your stories, chapters and comments are deleted with it. Your forum posts stay, shown under “Deleted user”. Before you go, you can download your data and your stories.

If we suspend or close your account. We may suspend or close an account that seriously or repeatedly breaks these terms or the Community guidelines, or the law. We tell you why and how to appeal. While your account is suspended, you can still sign in, read and download your data, but you can’t post, comment or message. If we close your account, you can’t sign up again with the same email address.

If we close {{productName}}. If we ever have to stop running {{productName}}, we will tell you at least [number] days before, so you can download your data and your stories.

## 10. Our responsibility, complaints and the law

**In short** (proposed)

- We work hard to keep {{productName}} safe and running, but we can’t promise it will never break.
- We’re responsible for harm we cause, as the law says.
- If something goes wrong, tell us first. You can also go to a court or an authority.

**Full text**

What we promise. We work to keep {{productName}} safe, private and running, and to follow these terms. We can’t promise that {{productName}} will always be available or free of mistakes, and we may change or remove features. If we remove a feature you rely on, we’ll tell you in good time.

What we’re responsible for. We’re responsible for harm we cause by breaking these terms or the law, as the law says. Nothing in these terms limits our responsibility where the law doesn’t allow it, for example for harm we cause on purpose or through serious carelessness, or your rights as a consumer. We’re not responsible for what other people post, but we act on reports as these terms explain.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Other services. Some things on {{productName}} come from other services, such as music and video you choose to play, or signing in with Google, Apple or Microsoft. Their own terms and age rules apply when you use them.

Complaints. If something goes wrong, please tell us first through Contact us in Settings or at {{contactEmail}}. We answer in Romanian or English. For decisions about content or accounts, you can also use the appeal routes in our Notice and action policy. If you’re not happy with how we handled a complaint, you can contact [consumer protection authority] or ANCOM, which supervises online platforms in Romania.

The law. These terms are governed by [Romanian law / Swedish law: depends on where the company is registered; lawyer to confirm]. If you live in the EU, you keep the protection of the mandatory laws of the country where you live, and you can go to court there.
