  legal_documents.id, '0.1', 'en', $legal_text$Privacy notice$legal_text$,
  $legal_text$## In short

- We collect as little as we can: your email, username, year of birth, your Yoti result (never the photo) and what you choose to post.
- We use it to run your account, keep people safe and follow the law. We never use it to choose ads and we don’t track you.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Under-16s need a parent’s or guardian’s consent.
- A few companies help us, mostly in the EU. Each one is named here.
- You can see, download, correct and delete your data. You can also complain to ANSPDCP.

## 1. Who looks after your data

**In short** (as on the board)

- [Company name] SRL or AB is responsible for your data.
- Questions about your data: {{privacyEmail}}.

**Full text**

[Company name] [SRL or AB], registered at [registered office], registration number [registration number], runs {{productName}}. We are the “controller” of your personal data. That means we decide how and why it is used, and we are responsible for looking after it.

How to reach us about your data: {{privacyEmail}}, or Contact us in Settings. You can write in Romanian or English.

Data protection officer: [OPEN: whether we must appoint one; if we do, their contact details go here].

[OPEN: if {{productName}} opens in the United Kingdom and the company has no office there, the name and address of our UK representative go here.]

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Class spaces. In a class space, the school is responsible for the information the teacher provides, such as class materials, grades and feedback. Pupils own their work, and we are responsible for everything else on {{productName}}. [Lawyer to confirm the roles of the school and of {{productName}}]

This notice may change. If we make an important change, we tell you on {{productName}} before it applies. Every version stays on this page with its date.

## 2. What we collect

**In short** (as on the board)

- Your email, password, year of birth (and birth month, if we needed it), username and pen name, if you add one. We store your password in a form nobody can read.
- What you write, comment and send in messages, and the pictures you upload.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} The result of your Yoti age check: passed or not. Never the photo.
- Your country, which we work out from your connection. We never ask for it.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} The county you pick, if you pick one.

*Suggested new short line, now that the full text fills the placeholder:*

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} The result of your Yoti age check, how it was done and when. Never the photo or the document.

**Full text**

We collect as little as we can. Here is everything.

What you give us:

- Your account: email address, password (we store it in a scrambled form nobody can read, not even us), year of birth, and your birth month only if we need it to work out whether you are under or over 13 or 16. We never ask for your full date of birth or your legal name.
- Your profile: username, pen name if you add one, bio and picture if you add them, interface language, the county or region you pick (optional) and the categories and tags you like.
- What you make: stories, chapters, drafts, comments, forum posts, private messages, pictures and drawings. Also the ratings and labels you give your stories.
- What you do: likes, follows, your library and where you are in a story, and private downvotes (only our staff can see these). Reads are counted per day as totals, not as a list of who read what.
- When you contact us: support messages, reports you make, appeals and data requests.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Optional extras: if you ask for a Teacher badge, your school email address or the proof you send (we delete the proof once we’ve checked it). If you ask for a writing certificate, the name you want printed on it.

What we get from others:

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Yoti: the result of your age check (“13 or over”, “18 or over” or failed), how it was done (selfie estimate, ID document or a check by a person) and the date. Never the photo, the document or an estimated age.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Your parent or guardian, if you’re under 16: their email address and their answer, with the date and the version of this notice they saw.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Google, Apple or Microsoft, if you sign in with them: your email address and, only if you allow it, your name. They don’t tell us your age.
- Your connection: when you sign up, we read your country from your internet connection and keep only the country code. For security we keep your IP address only in a scrambled form, for 30 days.
- Other people: reports about you or your content, and, in a class space, what your teacher adds.
- Authorities: orders and requests they send us.

Your user ID. Every account gets a random, permanent ID that links everything you make on {{productName}}. Nobody else ever sees it. You’ll find it in your own data downloads.

What we never collect: your legal name (unless you ask for a certificate), your full date of birth, your exact location, your contacts and any selfie or ID document.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} What you must give us. To create an account we need your email address, a password or sign-in link, your year of birth and an age check, and, if you’re under 16, your parent’s or guardian’s consent. Without these we can’t open an account for you. Everything else is up to you.

## 3. Why we use it

**In short** (as on the board)

- To run your account and show your work to readers.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} To keep people safe: age checks, parental consent and moderation.
- [The legal basis for each use: to be drafted by a Romanian lawyer]

*Suggested new short line, now that the full text fills the placeholder:*

- We use your data because you (or, under 16, your parent or guardian) agreed, because the law requires it or to keep {{productName}} safe. Each use and its reason is listed below.

**Full text**

The law says we need a valid reason, called a “legal basis”, for each way we use your data. Here is each use and its reason.

Running your account and showing your work. Creating your account, showing your stories, comments and posts to the readers your settings allow, messages, notifications, your library and your settings. Legal basis: your consent. If you’re under 16, your parent’s or guardian’s consent as well.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Checking your age. Your year of birth, the Yoti check and the protections that fit your age. Legal basis: your consent, which you give on Yoti’s consent screen before the check. [Lawyer to confirm whether explicit consent is needed for the selfie estimate]

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Parent or guardian consent for under-16s. Asking for it, recording it and checking a parent’s request against it. Legal basis: the law requires us to get it and to be able to show we got it.

Keeping people safe. Reading reports, checking every picture on upload, moderating, warnings and suspensions, statements of reasons and appeals. Legal basis: our legal duties under EU platform law, and our legitimate interest in keeping {{productName}} safe, especially for young people. [Lawyer to confirm the basis for the picture checks]

Protecting someone in danger. If someone’s life or safety is at risk, we may share what’s needed with the police or emergency services. Legal basis: our legal duty to report threats to life or safety, and protecting someone’s vital interests.

Orders from authorities. Acting on removal orders and other orders, and keeping removed content as the law requires. Legal basis: legal obligation.

Security and stopping spam. Scrambled IP addresses, sign-in limits and spam limits. Legal basis: our legitimate interest in keeping accounts and {{productName}} secure.

Your country. Reading your country from your connection, so that we only open {{productName}} where we’re ready and apply the right local rules. Legal basis: our legitimate interest.

Answering you. Support requests and data requests. Legal basis: your consent when you write to us, and our legal duty to answer requests about your data.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Teacher badges and certificates. Checking that you teach, and printing your name on a certificate you ask for. Legal basis: your consent.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Keeping forum posts after an account is deleted. So that conversations still make sense, posts stay under “Deleted user”. Legal basis: our legitimate interest in keeping discussions readable. You can ask us to remove personal information in them. [Lawyer to confirm]

Keeping records in case of a dispute. Keeping reports and decisions for a limited time after they close. Legal basis: our legitimate interest in being able to show what happened if a decision is challenged.

Counting what works. We count reads, sign-ups and returning readers as totals, without cookies and without tracking anyone. Legal basis: our legitimate interest.

Where we rely on legitimate interest, you can object, as explained in “Your rights”.

Withdrawing consent. You can withdraw your consent at any time, and it is as easy as giving it: [where in Settings: to be designed], or write to {{privacyEmail}}. Withdrawing doesn’t make what we did before unlawful. Because we need consent to run your account, [OPEN: what happens to the account, its stories and its posts when consent is withdrawn; lawyer to decide (L1)].

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Under 16. In Romania you can agree to your own data use from 16. Below that, a parent or guardian must agree too. We email them a link; until they agree, your account stays read-only. A parent or guardian can withdraw their consent at any time. Your account then goes read-only and we tell you both what happens next. [OPEN: how we check that the person is really a parent or guardian, beyond email (L2)]

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Protections for under-18s. If you’re under 18, your settings start at the most private choices. Adults can never message anyone under 16. If you’re 16 or 17, messages from adults are off unless you turn them on, and only you can do that. Nothing on {{productName}} tells an adult how old you are. You get no marketing emails, and quiet hours apply to notifications.

Automatic decisions. We don’t make decisions about you based only on automatic processing that have legal or similarly serious effects. Two things are partly automatic, and a person is always available:

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} The age check. Yoti’s selfie estimate decides which age group you’re in. If you disagree, you can use an ID document instead or ask for a check by one of our staff.
- The picture check. A flagged picture is only held back. A moderator decides what happens to it.

What we never do. We don’t sell your data. We don’t use it to choose ads, for anyone. We don’t build a profile of you to decide what you see.

## 4. Who else sees it

**In short** (as on the board)

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Yoti, for the age check.
- Sightengine and Arachnid Shield, which check every picture when it’s uploaded.
- [List of the companies that host and send things for us]
- We don’t use your data to choose ads.

*Suggested new short line, now that the full text fills the placeholder:*

- The companies that host {{productName}}, send our emails and run our help desk, each under a contract with us. Most are in the EU.

**Full text**

Other people on {{productName}}. What you publish is shown under your pen name or username to the readers your settings and ratings allow. The people you message see your messages. Space and class members see what you post in that space, and your teacher sees what you share with the class. Writers see their readers only as totals; below five readers they see “fewer than 5”. Nobody else sees your email address, your age, your year of birth or your age check result.

Our staff. Only staff who need it for their job, and each look is logged. They read private messages only when the messages are part of a report or the law requires it. During a manual age check, the staff member handling it sees your year of birth.

Companies that work for us. These companies handle data only on our instructions, under a contract that protects it (a data processing agreement):

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Yoti (age check). Yoti processes the selfie or document in its UK data centres, deletes the photo and document when the check is done, and keeps the result for up to 6 months.
- Sightengine (picture check for nudity and gore). [Where Sightengine processes pictures: to be confirmed]
- Arachnid Shield, run by the Canadian Centre for Child Protection (picture check against known child sexual abuse images). Pictures are checked in Canada.
- Supabase (our database, sign-in and file storage), in the EU (Ireland).
- Cloudflare (hosting for the website, through Cloudflare Pages).
- Brevo (sending our emails), in the EU.
- [Help desk provider, hosting data in the EU]
- [Error tracking provider, in the EU, with personal data removed]
- [Cookieless analytics provider, in the EU, once added]
- [Ad network, only if one is added: non-personalised ads only]

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Services you choose to use. When you sign in with Google, Apple or Microsoft, or press play on music or a video from Spotify, Apple Music, YouTube, Vimeo or Loom, that company gets data under its own privacy rules. Nothing loads from these services until you choose to use them. [After launch: Canva, if you connect it to design a cover]

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Schools. If you’re in a class space, the school receives the information the teacher handles for the class, such as grades and feedback.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Your parent or guardian. If you’re under 16, they receive the consent request and can ask for your data on your behalf.

Authorities. We share data with the police, prosecutors, courts, ANCOM or other authorities only when the law requires it, or when someone’s life or safety is at risk.

Advisers. Our lawyers and accountants, when they need it to advise us, under a duty of confidentiality.

Data outside the EU. Most of your data stays in the EU. Yoti processes age checks in the United Kingdom, and some of Yoti’s own suppliers are in India, the United States and Brazil. Arachnid Shield checks pictures in Canada. We only send data outside the EU where the European Commission has decided the country protects data properly, or under the Commission’s standard contract clauses. You can ask us for a copy of these safeguards at {{privacyEmail}}. [Lawyer to confirm the safeguard for each transfer]

Ads. We don’t use your data to choose ads, for anyone.

## 5. How long we keep it

**In short** (as on the board)

- [How long we keep each kind of data: to be set]
- When you delete your account, we delete your data and your stories, apart from what the law says we must keep. Your forum posts stay, shown as “Deleted user”.

*Suggested new short line, now that the full text fills the placeholder:*

- Each kind of data has its own time limit, listed below. Reports are kept for 3 years after they close.

**Full text**

We keep data only as long as we need it. Then our systems delete it automatically.

Your account

- Account and profile data: for as long as you have an account. When you delete your account, we switch it off straight away and delete it for good after 14 days, unless you change your mind.
- Your user ID is never given to anyone else, even after your account is deleted.
- Sign-ups that never finish: if you don’t confirm your email within 7 days, or no age check passes within 14 days, or a parent or guardian doesn’t agree within 14 days, we delete the account and everything in it. If a parent or guardian says no, we delete it straight away.
- If you’re under 13, we don’t keep anything you typed.

Age check

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Your result, its method and its date: for as long as you have an account.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} The temporary link between your account and your Yoti session: deleted as soon as the result arrives.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} At Yoti: the photo and document are deleted when the check is done; the result is kept for up to 6 months.

Parent or guardian consent

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} The consent record (who agreed, when, which email it went to and which version of this notice): for as long as the account exists. [OPEN: how long after the account is deleted we keep it as proof of consent]

What you make

- Stories, chapters, comments and pictures: until you delete them, or until your account is deleted. Deleted items are removed from our systems within 30 days.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Forum posts: they stay after your account is deleted, shown under “Deleted user”, until a moderator or the space removes them.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Private messages: deleted once both people have deleted the conversation.
- Copies in backups: overwritten within [number] days.

Safety

- Reports, including the name and email of the person who reported: 3 years after the report is closed, then deleted.
- Moderation decisions, statements of reasons and appeals: [OPEN: proposal, 3 years after the case closes, the same as reports]
- Reported private messages: [OPEN: proposal, 3 years after the case closes, the same as reports]
- Content removed after an order to remove terrorist content: kept for 6 months, as the law requires, unless an authority asks for longer.
- Content under an open legal case: kept until the case ends.
- Evidence kept for the police in a child safety case: [OPEN: to be set with the lawyer]

Security

- IP addresses: scrambled, then deleted after 30 days.
- The list of devices you’re signed in on: [OPEN: to be set]

Requests and support

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Your data download files: deleted 7 days after they’re ready.
- Records of data requests (what was asked, when, the identity check and what we sent, but not the files): [OPEN: to be set]
- Support requests: [OPEN: proposal, 2 years after the request is closed]

Optional extras

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Teacher badge proof: deleted as soon as we’ve checked it. We keep only how you asked, our decision, the date and who decided. [OPEN: whether we keep your school email address or only its domain]
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Certificates: deleted when your account is deleted. The certificate’s code then shows “no longer available”.

Records the law makes us keep

- Invoices and accounting records for sponsors and schools that pay us: [5 years from 1 July of the year after the financial year ends, under Romanian accounting law, if the company is a Romanian SRL; lawyer to confirm]

## 6. Your rights

**In short** (as on the board)

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} See your data and download it as a Word file from Settings.
- Correct it, delete it or object to how we use it.
- Complain to ANSPDCP, the Romanian data protection authority.

*Suggested new short line, now that the full text fills the placeholder:*

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} See your data and download it from Settings: a full copy you can open in other apps, plus your stories as Word files.

**Full text**

You have these rights over your data. Using them is free.

- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} See your data and get a copy. In Settings you can download everything you’ve put on {{productName}}, plus your account data and your user ID, in a format other apps can read. You can also download your stories as Word files, one per story or all at once.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Take your data elsewhere. The same download gives you your data in a form you can move to another service.
- Correct it. You can change most things yourself in Settings. For anything else, ask us.
- {{afterTheInvitedTest}} Delete it. You can delete your account and its data from Settings, on any device or on the website. You can also delete single stories, comments and posts at any time.
- Limit how we use it, for example while we check a complaint you’ve made.
- Object to how we use it, where we rely on our legitimate interest. We’ll stop, unless we have strong reasons the law accepts.
- Withdraw your consent at any time. See “Why we use it”.
- Ask for a person to look again at a decision made with an automatic tool, such as the age check.

How to ask. Use Settings, Contact us or {{privacyEmail}}. We answer within one month. If a request is complicated, we may need up to two more months, and we’ll tell you within the first month. If you don’t ask from inside your signed-in account, we first check that you are who you say you are, by a link to your account’s email address or by asking you to sign in. We never send your data to any other address.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} If you’re 13 to 15. You can download your own data from your own account. A parent or guardian can also ask on your behalf; we check them against the consent record first.

Complaints. If you think we’ve got something wrong, please tell us first. You can always complain to ANSPDCP, the Romanian data protection authority (www.dataprotection.ro), or to the data protection authority where you live or work. [If the company is a Swedish AB: add IMY, the Swedish data protection authority, and confirm the lead authority]

## 7. Cookies and storage

**In short** (as on the board)

- No tracking cookies.
- We store only what the app needs to work on your device, such as keeping you logged in. That’s why there’s no cookie banner.

**Full text**

We don’t use tracking cookies, and we don’t let anyone else track you through {{productName}}.

What we store on your device. Only what {{productName}} needs to work, because you asked to use it:

- your sign-in, so you stay logged in;
- your language choice;
- that you closed a notice at the top of the page (for that visit only).

[Exact list of cookies and stored items, with names and how long each lasts: to be added from the technical check before launch]

What we don’t store. No advertising cookies, no analytics cookies and nothing that follows you across other websites. Our visitor counts are made without cookies.

Because everything we store is needed for {{productName}} to work, the law doesn’t require us to ask first. That’s why there’s no cookie banner.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Music and video. Songs and videos from other services load only when you press play. Until then, nothing loads from them. When you press play, that service may use its own cookies. For YouTube we use its privacy-enhanced player.

{{afterTheInvitedTest}} Ads. Ad-network code must not set cookies or read anything stored on your device. If that ever had to change, we’d ask you first, and you could say no.

Every new script or stored item is checked before it goes live, so this list stays true.
